{{nihongo|'''Shishido Hayashi'''|林 宍戸|Hayashi Shishido}} was a Sanese bureaucrat and military officer who served as the 15th [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from April to October 1933. A conservative bureaucrat and member of the House of Peers, Hayashi's "national unity" government, supported by the military, oversaw the worst years of the Depression. His administration was paralyzed by the growing independence of the military from civilian control, as radical officers exploited the [[Saito Constitution]]'s loopholes to operate beyond the Diet's authority. His brief, ineffective premiership demonstrated the impossibility of governing when the military operated as a state within the state.

== Early life ==
Shishido Hayashi was born on 22 April 1874 in [[Hirogawa Prefecture|Hirogawa]], the son of a senior bureaucrat in the Home Ministry. The Hayashi family belonged to the professional administrative class that had emerged from the [[Saito Restoration]]—not samurai by birth, but educated officials who had built their careers in the new government.

Hayashi attended the prefectural school in Hirogawa before entering the Imperial Army Academy in 1890. He was commissioned as a junior officer in 1894 and served in the infantry for twelve years, rising to the rank of major. His military career was competent but undistinguished; he was regarded as a reliable administrator rather than a brilliant strategist.

In 1906, Hayashi transferred from active military service to the Army Ministry's bureaucracy, where he oversaw procurement and logistics. His administrative skills earned him promotion to the senior ranks of the military civil service, and by 1920 he had been appointed Vice Minister of the Army.

Hayashi's transition from military officer to civilian bureaucrat was completed in 1925, when he was appointed to the House of Peers—the upper chamber of the Diet that represented the aristocracy and the senior bureaucracy. As a member of the House of Peers, Hayashi became known as a conservative voice who supported the military's prerogatives while advocating for fiscal discipline and administrative efficiency.

== Political career ==
Hayashi's political career was defined by his position at the intersection of the military and the civilian bureaucracy. He was not a party politician—he viewed the party system with the same suspicion that most military officers felt—but he was also not a radical militarist. He believed in a strong state, a powerful military, and an efficient bureaucracy, and he was willing to work within the constitutional framework to achieve these goals.

During the late 1920s and early 1930s, Hayashi served on several government commissions tasked with addressing the economic Depression and the growing political crisis. He developed a reputation as a competent administrator who could manage complex bureaucratic challenges, but he was also seen as a figure who would not challenge the military's autonomy.

When [[Takaaki Kato]] died in office on 5 April 1933, the genrō council and the military leadership agreed that Hayashi was the best candidate to succeed him. His appointment was supported by the Army, which saw him as a reliable ally, and by the conservative elements of the bureaucracy, who viewed him as a competent administrator. The political parties, weakened by the collapse of the Go-Shōkō Democracy, had little influence over the selection process.

== Premiership (1933) ==
Hayashi was appointed Prime Minister on 5 April 1933. His "national unity" government was explicitly designed to bridge the gap between the military and the civilian bureaucracy, bringing together Army officers, senior bureaucrats, and a few token party politicians in a cabinet that was dominated by the military establishment.

=== The Depression ===
[[File:Hayashi Depression Austerity 1933.png|thumb|right|200px|Depression-era unemployment and austerity during the Hayashi government of 1933]]
The Hayashi government took office during the worst year of the economic Depression. Unemployment had reached 20% in the industrial cities, agricultural prices had collapsed, and the banking system was on the verge of failure. The government's response was hampered by the military's insistence on maintaining high levels of defense spending, which consumed resources that might otherwise have been directed toward economic relief.

Hayashi attempted to implement a program of fiscal austerity, cutting non-military spending while preserving the defense budget. The austerity measures were deeply unpopular, provoking protests in the industrial cities and further eroding public confidence in the government. The Depression worsened throughout Hayashi's brief tenure, and the government was unable to implement any meaningful economic recovery program.

=== Military independence ===
The defining feature of the Hayashi government was the military's growing independence from civilian control. Radical Army officers, organized in secret societies such as the Cherry Blossom Society ({{nihongo|Sakurakai|桜会}}), 
[[File:Hayashi Military Independence 1933.png|thumb|right|200px|Radical Army officers exploited the Saito Constitution's loopholes to operate beyond the Diet's authority]]
exploited the [[Saito Constitution]]'s loopholes to operate beyond the Diet's authority. The Constitution's provisions on independent command ({{nihongo|tōsuiken|統帥権}}) were interpreted ever more broadly, placing not only military operations but also strategic planning, troop deployments, and even foreign policy decisions beyond the cabinet's purview.

Hayashi, as a former Army officer himself, was unable or unwilling to challenge this interpretation. When junior Army officers independently authorized the expansion of military operations in the [[Shendan]] region in the summer of 1933, Hayashi protested but took no action to discipline the officers involved. His failure to assert civilian authority was widely seen as confirmation that the prime minister had become a figurehead, unable to control the military establishment.

The government was further paralyzed by factional conflicts within the Army itself. The radical "Imperial Way" faction ({{nihongo|Kōdōha|皇道派}}), which advocated for a direct military takeover of the government, was increasingly at odds with the moderate "Control" faction ({{nihongo|Tōseiha|統制派}}), which favored maintaining the existing constitutional framework while expanding military influence. Hayashi, who was aligned with the Tōseiha, was unable to prevent the Kōdōha from pursuing its own agenda through acts of political violence and intimidation.

=== Collapse ===
By October 1933, the Hayashi government had become completely paralyzed. The cabinet was unable to pass significant legislation, the military was operating without civilian oversight, and the economy continued to deteriorate. Hayashi himself was increasingly isolated, unable to command the support of either the military or the political parties.

Hayashi resigned on 14 October 1933, after only six months in office. He was succeeded by [[Reijiro Wakatsuki]], who was appointed as another compromise candidate but would prove equally unable to govern in the face of military opposition.

=== Foreign policy ===
The Hayashi government's foreign policy was effectively dictated by the military. The Army's independent expansion of operations in the Shendan in the summer of 1933 was the most significant foreign policy development of the brief administration, and it occurred without the prime minister's authorization. The unauthorized deployments further inflamed tensions with the Illypnian colonial powers, particularly Volisania, which had been imposing increasingly restrictive trade regulations on Sanese commercial vessels.

The government made no significant diplomatic initiatives of its own. Hayashi's attempts to pursue a negotiated settlement of the Shendan trade disputes were blocked by the Army, which refused to provide the diplomatic concessions that would have been necessary for meaningful negotiations. The Echian-Sanese relationship was maintained through routine diplomatic channels, but no new agreements were negotiated during Hayashi's tenure.

The Kwangju relationship continued to deepen under military direction, with the Army expanding the advisory mission and authorizing new economic concessions without cabinet approval. The Shansian government protested these developments through diplomatic notes, but the Hayashi government was unable to respond coherently, as the Army's Kwangju policy was being formulated independently of the cabinet.

=== Domestic policy beyond austerity ===
Beyond the fiscal austerity program, the Hayashi government's domestic policy was characterized by paralysis and drift. The government introduced no significant social legislation, and the existing labor protections and welfare programs were effectively suspended as the austerity measures reduced government spending across all non-military categories.

The government did, however, introduce the National Mobilization Preparatory Act of 1933, which authorized the government to begin planning for the total mobilization of the economy in the event of a national emergency. The Act was presented as a precautionary measure, but it established the legal framework that would later be used by the Taisei Yokusankai to impose wartime economic controls. The Act's passage, with minimal Diet debate, was a sign of the legislature's growing impotence in the face of military-dominated government.

== Legacy ==
Shishido Hayashi is remembered as one of the least effective prime ministers in Okaiken's history. His brief, paralyzed administration demonstrated the impossibility of governing when the military operated as a state within the state. His failure to assert civilian authority over the military—even as a former Army officer himself—confirmed that the Saito constitutional order had broken down beyond repair.

Hayashi's premiership is also significant for what it revealed about the internal dynamics of the military. The conflict between the Kōdōha and Tōseiha factions, which paralyzed his government, would continue to escalate in subsequent years, culminating in the series of military uprisings and assassinations that would define the politics of the late 1930s.

After his resignation, Hayashi retired from public life. He died in [[Okami]] on 15 March 1948 at the age of 73, having lived to see the end of the war and the beginning of the post-war reconstruction.

